Compatibility planning:

- Prioritize the v2 scope/design in XORCYST_V2_DESIGN.md. Requirements
  must trace to an owner decision, an existing project dependency, or a concrete
  defect; synthetic probes do not automatically justify new language features.
  D0 was approved on 2026-10-22 and D1 on 2026-10-23 after independent review.
  I1's isolated value graph in v2/value.c received owner approval on 2026-10-23
  after local independent review found no blockers; see v2/README.md.
  I2 layout/encoding is implemented with direct-IR tests and local validation.
  Independent local review cleared I2's runtime at 92b5243 and coverage at
  35fbb0c with no findings. The owner approved I2 on 2026-10-24 for publication
  as one PR and CI; see v2/README.md. Later project and cutover gates stay open.
  I2 merged as PR #98 (2083c7a). I3 now has an unpublished direct-binary
  source prototype, source controls and a plain-project benchmark harness.
  Its review follow-up fixes declaration identity across .undef, nested-macro
  binding and RAM truncation warnings. Source-time sizeof/PC width assumptions
  are also rechecked when final-name binding changes an operand's kind or
  string length. Backward local labels keep their fixed data width. The S1/R1
  follow-up includes the owner-approved sizeof reassignment clarification.
  Size guards bind the actual data-line capture by walking surviving native
  statements after pass 1, including operands later in the sizeof use's own
  statement and anonymous +/- references whose renaming finishes later.
  Repeated sizeof operands count their integer width without re-entering the
  same scan; their complete expressions still undergo the kind check.
  Leading labels that share a data run share this rule. The owner explicitly
  approved rejecting self-sized strings on 2026-10-24; the contract ledger
  and NEWS record that compatibility choice independently of reassignment.
  Unprocessed user-typed initializers that are flattened/freed leave an unbound
  guard and give a located size-unknown error. No guard retains an AST statement.
  Independent local review cleared I3 at 7ffb2ac with no defects or blockers.
  The coverage follow-up adds .exitm tombstone controls without runtime changes;
  owner approval is still required before its PR. The owner permits a memory
  increase below 30% over matched v1; all no-slowdown and later analysis gates still apply.
  Object/link and analysis/consumer gates remain I4/I5 work.
  The owner-approved work-bound correction is in contract section 10; the
  project no-slowdown and byte-exact ROM gates are unchanged.
  Complete local independent review before publishing a branch or opening/updating
  a PR; keep one PR at a time.
  Keep optional v1 repairs separate. No current formats change in this plan.
- Optional I3 follow-ups: very long shared-label sizeof runs repeatedly scan
  their leading labels (cubic work in synthetic cases); collect those labels
  once per scan if this becomes a measured project cost. The reviewed 800/1600
  label cases took about 0.41/6.6 seconds, versus v1's 0.015/0.04 seconds;
  neither Metroid nor Zelda uses sizeof. Multiple sizeof guards can also print
  the same size-unknown error repeatedly. Both remain non-blocking review notes.
- D1's approved contracts and approval record are in XORCYST_V2_CONTRACTS.md. Required ROM
  baselines and Metroid/Zelda analysis captures are frozen; see
  design/v2/BASELINES.md. Semantics, the no-slowdown measurement rule and the
  concrete object encoding are reviewed and approved; retain their gates
  throughout implementation.
  Freeze converter outputs for gate builds: Wishmaster xm2nes output varies
  with the environment. Keep baseline archives outside the repository.
  The owner chose median <= 1.000 with no regression confirmed in an independent
  session; see design/v2/PERFORMANCE.md for the full decision rule.
- v2 may replace the object format without reading or writing v1 objects.
  Use that freedom to remove demonstrated encoding limitations, not to redesign
  working source binding, placement, or every analysis field. Rebuild objects
  and coordinate consumer migration at cutover.
- Aim for one structured-listing schema with an output identifier, not
  indefinite selectable legacy schemas. Start from existing complete listing
  version 2 facts and preserve working conventions. Migrate Nesrev before
  removing --listing-version. The approved scope removes object-mode structured
  listing/xref exports; retain text listing only through the shared renderer.
  Listing version 2 currently requires pure binary; that is not a statement
  about a future XORcyst software version 2. New xlnk analysis is not required.
- Full forward-string data support is an explicit v2 request: final extents,
  use-site character maps, existing string operations and matching analysis.
  The selected v1 policy is consistent rejection of unresolved string data;
  that narrow repair and exported-alias repairs remain separate work.
- v2 must be no slower than v1 on large projects such as Metroid, both plain
  and with listing, xref, records and actual combined Nesrev analysis profiles.
  Include object-mode xasm, xlnk and full builds of bombsweeper, dpadhero2,
  ntxdev and the projects under nesdev/music. The selected dpadhero tree is
  /Users/kenthansen/Dropbox/nesdev/ntxdev/games/dpadhero (build in src), but
  its full-ROM test is non-blocking while it depends on precompiled v1 song
  objects. Do not spend work recovering/converting them or supporting v1 objects.
  Require byte-identical ROMs;
  the working dpadhero2/src build is a mandatory acceptance gate. Freeze actual
  source/assets/tools, including local inputs, without changing their trees.
  Improvement is the goal. The v1 5% plus 20 ms allowance is not a v2 budget;
  investigate noise and gate each configuration, not an average across modes.
- The approved v2 scope selects xlnk's relaxation loop on both paths,
  rejects unresolved PCs in expansion directives and PC-dependent field
  dimensions, and decides the known binary/link differences in section 3.5.
  These are approved scope decisions, not changes to the current v1 behavior.

Known defects and follow-ups:

- Late word instruction operands outside -32768..65535 still warn and truncate
  in pure-binary output while the linker rejects them. Decide the policy together
  with 16-bit address wrapping: existing binaries/listings can cross $FFFF. Late
  byte operands now have matching assembler/linker range checks.
- FCEUX ROM export rejects an unevaluable or negative storage count during
  analysis collection, before the writer can supply its diagnostic listing.
  A late negative count can lose the located range error too, leaving only
  "could not collect assembly analysis" and exit 3 without a manifest. With a
  dependency manifest this also exits 3 when an invalid shift has already
  produced a located error. Omit ROM export to obtain the ordinary diagnostic.
  Deferring collection failure also needs safe destination planning: unknown
  size means not all ROM bank paths can be enumerated, and allowing a listing
  can overwrite an NL file that was omitted from the incomplete plan.
- An omitted structure-array count that still depends on $ after constant
  substitution uses first-element expansion and cannot become zero in
  pure-binary mode. Object output retains its previous link-time behavior.
  The binary remainder evaluates the count after the first element, changing
  some layouts from 1.9.2, which evaluated it at the field start. Constants
  and label differences support late zero counts, including nested layouts.
- PC equates and PC-relative .org now reject a first-pass position made
  uncertain by deferred storage. Other first-pass consumers of $ (conditionals,
  loops and messages),
  later instruction shortening and reassigned type dimensions still need a
  general final-layout/capture policy. Use labels for final addresses.
  Consider resolving deferred PC equates instead of conservatively rejecting
  them, including recomputing prefixes after a count becomes known.
- Both outer omitted-array counts and nested field counts that depend on $
  can differ between binary and object output: binary uses the remainder's
  PC after the first element for an outer count and each field's PC for nested
  counts; object merging uses the start of the merged storage. At origin zero,
  an omitted Inner[3-$] with two-byte elements and a trailing byte occupies
  three bytes in binary, seven in object output and in 1.9.2. Keep address-
  dependent field counts in labels/constants when both output modes must agree.
- Conditional extents for late omitted structure-array counts add expression
  evaluation work, especially for nested arrays and object output. Benchmark
  omitted arrays too, not only explicitly initialized arrays, when reducing
  this overhead. Known positive counts use direct expansion.
- Shared alias chains and dependent scoped offsets that remain unresolved
  (for example, with label or external counts) are expanded as trees, so
  evaluation time and object size can grow exponentially. Completed integer
  values are cached per evaluation. Sufficiently deep definitions can also
  exhaust the recursive evaluator's stack.
- Repeated cyclic references within one ordinary expression can report once
  per use (e.g. .byte Value+Value), rather than once per expression.
- Invalid dotted storage counts can report the same error for both the count
  and byte-size expressions, followed by a range error. Cyclic counts in
  uninitialized structures can also report at both instance and declaration.
- Union dimensions must be known when the union is declared.
- A field remainder that becomes zero only at link time is encoded as storage
  and rejected by the linker instead of being omitted.
- A forward code-label difference used as storage size can assemble in binary
  mode but fail object link layout with "storage size must evaluate to an
  integer during layout". For example, .dsb Tail-Head before Head: nop / nop /
  Tail: rts emits two zero bytes in binary. Support resolvable forward
  differences during link layout. Listing v2 already describes the full fill
  span with byte_count and fill_byte; text/v1 intentionally omit storage
  bytes while retaining following addresses and output offsets.
- Data-segment storage counts built from data-segment labels can fail link
  layout with "storage size must evaluate to an integer during layout", even
  when the link script provides a RAM region.
- A late error in merged storage can point to the preceding storage statement
  instead of the statement that supplied the invalid count.
- Forward strings differ between binary and object output, and exported forward
  constant aliases can still fail object encoding.
